<style>
  .text-columns {
    column-width: 200px;
    column-gap: var(--space-m);
    column-rule: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-dark) 50%, var(--color-light));
    margin-top: var(--space-l);
    border: 1px solid currentColor;
    padding: var(--space-m);

    @media (min-width: 640px) {
      .text-columns { column-count: 3; }
    }
    p {
      margin: 0 0 var(--space-2xs);
      font-size: 0.85rem;
    }

    .intro {
      column-span: all;
      color: var(--color-accent);
      font-weight: 600;
      margin-bottom: var(--space-s);
      font-size: 1rem;
    }
  }
</style>

<div class="wrapper stacked">

  <section class="demo-section">

    <div class="text-columns">
      <p class="intro">This line spans all columns first, then the rest of the content flows into separate columns below it.</p>
      <p>Without changing the parent column setup, one child can opt out of the column flow and sit full-width as an intro.</p>
      <p>This works well for lead paragraphs, section labels, and short contextual notes before dense text content.</p>
      <p>After that line, normal column flow resumes and the browser continues distributing paragraphs by available space.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
